That's right.

196
00:10:14,220 --> 00:10:16,340
And today we're going to talk about something

197
00:10:16,340 --> 00:10:18,660
that almost everyone struggles with

198
00:10:18,660 --> 00:10:20,740
at some point in their career.

199
00:10:20,740 --> 00:10:25,300
Yes, today's topic is how to beat procrastination at work.

200
00:10:25,300 --> 00:10:28,060
This is a very practical and important topic

201
00:10:28,060 --> 00:10:33,460
because procrastination can affect productivity, performance, and even confidence.

202
00:10:33,460 --> 00:10:38,420
Exactly. And many people think procrastination is just about laziness,

203
00:10:38,420 --> 00:10:43,580
but in reality, it's often about fear, confusion, or lack of clarity.

204
00:10:43,580 --> 00:10:48,900
That's a great point. Sometimes people delay tasks not because they don't want to do them,

205
00:10:48,900 --> 00:10:51,220
but because they don't know how to start.

206
00:10:51,220 --> 00:10:55,940
So today, we're going to walk you through a realistic, detailed conversation

207
00:10:55,940 --> 00:10:58,700
where we explore why procrastination happens

208
00:10:58,700 --> 00:11:01,380
and how to deal with it step by step.

209
00:11:01,380 --> 00:11:04,500
We'll include practical examples, natural English,

210
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:08,220
and useful phrases that you can use in your daily work life.

211
00:11:08,220 --> 00:11:11,140
And remember, the goal is not to become perfect,

212
00:11:11,140 --> 00:11:12,580
but to become more aware

213
00:11:12,580 --> 00:11:15,260
and take small actions consistently.

214
00:11:15,260 --> 00:11:16,200
Exactly.

215
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:17,620
So let's begin.

216
00:11:17,620 --> 00:11:19,420
Imagine this situation.

217
00:11:19,420 --> 00:11:21,140
Sophia is a manager,

218
00:11:21,140 --> 00:11:23,540
and I'm an employee who has been struggling

219
00:11:23,540 --> 00:11:25,100
with procrastination.

220
00:11:25,100 --> 00:11:26,900
Let's start the conversation.

221
00:11:26,900 --> 00:11:29,660
Hi, Daniel, do you have a few minutes to talk?

222
00:11:29,660 --> 00:11:30,620
Hi, Sophia.

223
00:11:30,620 --> 00:11:31,900
Yes, of course.

224
00:11:31,900 --> 00:11:34,740
I wanted to check in with you about a few deadlines.

225
00:11:34,740 --> 00:11:37,940
I noticed that some of your recent tasks were delayed.

226
00:11:37,940 --> 00:11:39,300
Is everything okay?

227
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:40,620
Thank you for asking.

228
00:11:40,620 --> 00:11:42,720
To be honest, I've been struggling a bit

229
00:11:42,720 --> 00:11:46,220
with staying focused and starting tasks on time.

230
00:11:46,220 --> 00:11:47,940
I appreciate your honesty.

231
00:11:47,940 --> 00:11:49,380
That's the first step.

232
00:11:49,380 --> 00:11:52,320
Can you tell me more about what's causing the delay?

233
00:11:52,320 --> 00:11:54,500
I think it's a combination of things.

234
00:11:54,500 --> 00:11:58,660
Sometimes the tasks feel overwhelming, and I don't know where to start.

235
00:11:58,660 --> 00:12:00,660
Other times I get distracted easily.

236
00:12:00,660 --> 00:12:02,660
That's very common.

237
00:12:02,660 --> 00:12:03,820
Let's break it down.

238
00:12:03,820 --> 00:12:07,060
Can you give me an example of a task you delayed recently?

239
00:12:07,060 --> 00:12:08,060
Yes.

240
00:12:08,060 --> 00:12:10,860
Last week I had to prepare a detailed report.

241
00:12:10,860 --> 00:12:13,900
I kept postponing it until the last moment.

242
00:12:13,900 --> 00:12:16,660
What made that task difficult to start?

243
00:12:16,660 --> 00:12:18,060
It felt too big.

244
00:12:18,060 --> 00:12:21,740
There were too many sections, and I wasn't sure how to organize everything.

245
00:12:21,740 --> 00:12:24,020
That's a classic situation.

246
00:12:24,020 --> 00:12:27,620
When a task feels too big, our brain tends to avoid it.

247
00:12:27,620 --> 00:12:30,340
Yes, that's exactly how I felt.

248
00:12:30,340 --> 00:12:35,220
So one effective strategy is to break the task into smaller parts.

249
00:12:35,220 --> 00:12:40,540
Instead of thinking, I need to write a report, you can think, I will write the introduction

250
00:12:40,540 --> 00:12:41,540
first.

251
00:12:41,540 --> 00:12:43,500
That sounds much more manageable.

252
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:44,500
Exactly.

253
00:12:44,500 --> 00:12:47,340
Let's try applying that to your report.

254
00:12:47,340 --> 00:12:50,060
What could be the first small step?

255
00:12:50,060 --> 00:12:52,340
Maybe outlining the main sections?

256
00:12:52,340 --> 00:12:53,540
Perfect.

257
00:12:53,540 --> 00:12:59,940
after that? Then I could write just one section at a time. That's a great approach. Now let's talk

258
00:12:59,940 --> 00:13:07,060
about another issue you mentioned, distractions. What kind of distractions do you face? Mostly my phone,

259
00:13:07,060 --> 00:13:13,300
emails, and sometimes social media. That's very common in modern workplaces. What do you usually

260
00:13:13,300 --> 00:13:18,340
do when you get distracted? I tell myself I'll check something quickly, but then I lose track

261
00:13:18,340 --> 00:13:24,900
of time. That happens to many people. One technique you can use is time blocking. What do you mean

262
00:13:24,900 --> 00:13:31,460
by time blocking? It means setting a specific period of time to focus on one task without

263
00:13:31,460 --> 00:13:38,340
interruptions. That sounds helpful. How long should the time block be? You can start with 25 minutes,

264
00:13:38,340 --> 00:13:43,540
focus completely during that time, then take a short break. That seems doable.

265
00:13:43,540 --> 00:13:47,140
Yes, it's often called the focus and break method.

266
00:13:47,140 --> 00:13:49,740
It helps train your brain to stay focused.

267
00:13:49,740 --> 00:13:51,240
I like that idea.

268
00:13:51,240 --> 00:13:54,240
Now let's imagine a real work situation.

269
00:13:54,240 --> 00:13:55,040
Sure.

270
00:13:55,040 --> 00:13:58,840
You arrive at work and you have three important tasks.

271
00:13:58,840 --> 00:14:00,840
What would you do differently now?

272
00:14:00,840 --> 00:14:05,040
First, I would list the tasks and break them into smaller steps.

273
00:14:05,040 --> 00:14:05,840
Good.

274
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:09,040
Then I would prioritize them based on importance.

275
00:14:09,040 --> 00:14:10,040
Excellent.

276
00:14:10,040 --> 00:14:14,840
After that, I would start with one task and set a 25-minute focus timer.

277
00:14:14,840 --> 00:14:16,600
That's a strong plan.

278
00:14:16,600 --> 00:14:17,600
Thank you.

279
00:14:17,600 --> 00:14:19,440
Let's go deeper.

280
00:14:19,440 --> 00:14:22,640
Sometimes procrastination is not just about tasks.

281
00:14:22,640 --> 00:14:24,960
It can also be about emotions.

282
00:14:24,960 --> 00:14:27,840
Do you ever feel anxious about certain tasks?

283
00:14:27,840 --> 00:14:32,800
Yes, especially when the task is important or when I'm afraid of making mistakes.

284
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:35,720
That's very important to recognize.

285
00:14:35,720 --> 00:14:38,600
Fear of failure can lead to procrastination.

286
00:14:38,600 --> 00:14:39,760
That makes sense.

287
00:14:39,760 --> 00:14:43,840
So in those situations, it helps to change your mindset.

288
00:14:43,840 --> 00:14:47,400
Instead of aiming for perfection, aim for progress.

289
00:14:47,400 --> 00:14:50,320
So just start even if it's not perfect.

290
00:14:50,320 --> 00:14:51,440
Exactly.

291
00:14:51,440 --> 00:14:53,520
Starting is often the hardest part.

292
00:14:53,520 --> 00:14:54,600
I agree.

293
00:14:54,600 --> 00:14:57,760
Once I start, it becomes easier to continue.

294
00:14:57,760 --> 00:15:01,560
That's because your brain shifts from resistance to action.

295
00:15:01,560 --> 00:15:02,560
That's interesting.

296
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:05,400
Let's practice a real work situation.

297
00:15:05,400 --> 00:15:06,400
Sure.

298
00:15:06,400 --> 00:15:10,800
you need to write an email, but you keep delaying it. What would you do?

299
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:16,160
I would start by writing a simple first draft without worrying too much about perfection.

300
00:15:16,160 --> 00:15:16,800
Great.

301
00:15:16,800 --> 00:15:18,640
Then I would revise it after.

302
00:15:18,640 --> 00:15:20,080
That's the right approach.

303
00:15:20,080 --> 00:15:22,240
Yes, that feels less stressful.

304
00:15:22,240 --> 00:15:26,960
Now let's talk about deadlines. Do deadlines help you or stress you?

305
00:15:26,960 --> 00:15:31,360
Both. They help me focus, but sometimes I wait until the last minute.

306
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:37,760
That's a common pattern. One solution is to create personal deadlines before the actual deadline.

307
00:15:37,760 --> 00:15:39,040
That's a good idea.

308
00:15:39,040 --> 00:15:44,000
For example, if something is due on Friday, you can aim to finish it by Wednesday.

309
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,320
That would reduce last-minute stress.

310
00:15:46,320 --> 00:15:47,200
Exactly.

311
00:15:47,200 --> 00:15:48,400
I'll try that.

312
00:15:48,400 --> 00:15:51,840
Now, let's imagine another workplace situation.

313
00:15:51,840 --> 00:15:52,560
Sure.

314
00:15:52,560 --> 00:15:57,040
You're assigned a complex project. What would be your first step?

315
00:15:57,040 --> 00:16:01,200
I would clarify the requirements to make sure I understand the task.

316
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:02,680
That's very important.

317
00:16:02,680 --> 00:16:05,360
Then I would break the project into faces.

318
00:16:05,360 --> 00:16:06,040
Good.

319
00:16:06,040 --> 00:16:08,720
And set small goals for each face.

320
00:16:08,720 --> 00:16:09,560
Excellent.

321
00:16:09,560 --> 00:16:13,200
I would also schedule regular check-ins to track progress.

322
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:15,440
That shows strong planning skills.

323
00:16:15,440 --> 00:16:16,200
Thank you.

324
00:16:16,200 --> 00:16:18,480
Now let's talk about motivation.

325
00:16:18,480 --> 00:16:21,040
What motivates you to complete tasks?

326
00:16:21,040 --> 00:16:24,320
Completing tasks and seeing progress motivates me.

327
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:25,400
That's great.

328
00:16:25,400 --> 00:16:29,920
You can use that by rewarding yourself after completing small steps.

329
00:16:29,920 --> 00:16:32,420
Like taking a break or having a coffee?

330
00:16:32,420 --> 00:16:33,420
Exactly.

331
00:16:33,420 --> 00:16:34,920
That sounds motivating.

332
00:16:34,920 --> 00:16:37,420
Now, let's address another issue.

333
00:16:37,420 --> 00:16:41,920
Sometimes people procrastinate because they don't find the task interesting.

334
00:16:41,920 --> 00:16:44,420
Yes, that happens to me sometimes.

335
00:16:44,420 --> 00:16:48,420
In that case, you can try connecting the task to a bigger goal.

336
00:16:48,420 --> 00:16:49,920
Can you give an example?

337
00:16:49,920 --> 00:16:52,920
For example, if you're doing a repetitive task,

338
00:16:52,920 --> 00:16:57,920
remind yourself that it contributes to your team's success or your career growth.

339
00:16:57,920 --> 00:17:00,300
That gives more meaning to the task.

340
00:17:00,300 --> 00:17:01,180
Exactly.

341
00:17:01,180 --> 00:17:02,220
That's helpful.

342
00:17:02,220 --> 00:17:05,020
Now let's simulate a deeper conversation,

343
00:17:05,020 --> 00:17:06,820
like in a performance review.

344
00:17:06,820 --> 00:17:07,540
Sure.

345
00:17:07,540 --> 00:17:10,620
Daniel, how do you plan to improve your productivity

346
00:17:10,620 --> 00:17:11,860
moving forward?

347
00:17:11,860 --> 00:17:14,900
I plan to break tasks into smaller steps,

348
00:17:14,900 --> 00:17:18,700
reduce distractions, and use time blocking techniques.

349
00:17:18,700 --> 00:17:20,220
That's a strong plan.

350
00:17:20,220 --> 00:17:22,260
I will also set personal deadlines

351
00:17:22,260 --> 00:17:24,780
and focus on starting tasks early.

352
00:17:24,780 --> 00:17:25,660
Excellent.

353
00:17:25,660 --> 00:17:28,100
and I will work on managing my mindset,

354
00:17:28,100 --> 00:17:30,380
especially when I feel overwhelmed.

355
00:17:30,380 --> 00:17:31,980
That's very important.

356
00:17:31,980 --> 00:17:32,820
Thank you.

357
00:17:32,820 --> 00:17:35,300
What do you think will be the biggest challenge?

358
00:17:35,300 --> 00:17:37,220
Staying consistent.

359
00:17:37,220 --> 00:17:38,380
That's honest.

360
00:17:38,380 --> 00:17:40,220
Consistency is key.

361
00:17:40,220 --> 00:17:42,620
Yes, I need to build better habits.

362
00:17:42,620 --> 00:17:43,460
Exactly.

363
00:17:43,460 --> 00:17:46,660
Small daily improvements lead to big results.

364
00:17:46,660 --> 00:17:47,660
I agree.

365
00:17:47,660 --> 00:17:50,700
Now let's move toward closing the conversation.

366
00:17:50,700 --> 00:17:51,540
Sure.

367
00:17:51,540 --> 00:17:54,780
Thank you for being open and discussing this honestly.

368
00:17:54,780 --> 00:17:56,460
Thank you for your guidance.

369
00:17:56,460 --> 00:17:59,380
I'm confident you can improve with these strategies.

370
00:17:59,380 --> 00:18:01,220
I will definitely apply them.

371
00:18:01,220 --> 00:18:02,060
Great.

372
00:18:02,060 --> 00:18:04,500
Now let's switch back to podcast mode.

373
00:18:04,500 --> 00:18:08,420
Today we explored how to beat procrastination at work.

374
00:18:08,420 --> 00:18:11,620
We talked about breaking tasks into smaller steps,

375
00:18:11,620 --> 00:18:14,560
managing distractions, and improving focus.

376
00:18:14,560 --> 00:18:17,340
We also discussed mindset, motivation,

377
00:18:17,340 --> 00:18:18,980
and practical techniques.

378
00:18:18,980 --> 00:18:22,540
Remember, procrastination is not a permanent problem.

379
00:18:22,540 --> 00:18:23,860
It's something you can improve

380
00:18:23,860 --> 00:18:25,700
with awareness and action.

381
00:18:25,700 --> 00:18:29,660
Skirt small, stay consistent, and focus on progress.

382
00:18:29,660 --> 00:18:32,660
Before we finish, here's a challenge for you.

383
00:18:32,660 --> 00:18:34,980
Think of one task you've been delaying.

384
00:18:34,980 --> 00:18:38,940
Break it into small steps and start the first step today.

385
00:18:38,940 --> 00:18:42,160
Even five minutes of action is better than waiting.

386
00:18:42,160 --> 00:18:45,180
The more you practice, the easier it becomes.

387
00:18:45,180 --> 00:18:47,340
Thank you so much for listening.

388
00:18:47,340 --> 00:18:49,540
Keep practicing, keep improving,

389
00:18:49,540 --> 00:18:51,380
and keep speaking English.

390
00:18:51,380 --> 00:18:52,980
See you in the next episode.

391
00:18:52,980 --> 00:18:54,380
Goodbye, everyone.

392
00:18:54,380 --> 00:18:58,060
Hello and welcome to Business English Conversations.

393
00:18:58,060 --> 00:18:58,900
Hello, everyone.

394
00:18:58,900 --> 00:19:02,300
Welcome back to the podcast where we practice real English

395
00:19:02,300 --> 00:19:03,460
for the workplace.

396
00:19:03,460 --> 00:19:04,620
My name is Marco.

397
00:19:04,620 --> 00:19:05,660
And I'm Erica.

398
00:19:05,660 --> 00:19:08,340
We're really happy you're here learning with us today.

399
00:19:08,340 --> 00:19:10,860
In this podcast, we help you improve your English

400
00:19:10,860 --> 00:19:13,580
through real conversations, practical examples,

401
00:19:13,580 --> 00:19:15,580
and professional situations.

402
00:19:15,580 --> 00:19:18,900
Yes, especially situations that are not always easy

403
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:22,300
to talk about, like productivity, time management,

404
00:19:22,300 --> 00:19:24,260
and workplace habits.

405
00:19:24,260 --> 00:19:29,660
And today's topic is something that affects almost everyone at some point in their career.

406
00:19:29,660 --> 00:19:30,660
That's right.

407
00:19:30,660 --> 00:19:34,780
Today we are going to talk about how to beat procrastination at work.

408
00:19:34,780 --> 00:19:39,760
This is not just about delaying tasks, it's about understanding why we procrastinate

409
00:19:39,760 --> 00:19:43,380
and learning how to take control of our time and energy.

410
00:19:43,380 --> 00:19:44,380
Exactly.

411
00:19:44,380 --> 00:19:47,780
And in today's conversation, we're going to go deeper than usual.

412
00:19:47,780 --> 00:19:53,220
We'll explore real workplace situations, practical strategies, and also the psychology

413
00:19:53,220 --> 00:19:55,420
behind procrastination.

414
00:19:55,420 --> 00:20:00,180
We'll also demonstrate how you can talk about these situations in English, especially in

415
00:20:00,180 --> 00:20:04,300
meetings, performance reviews, or conversations with your manager.

416
00:20:04,300 --> 00:20:05,740
So let's begin.

417
00:20:05,740 --> 00:20:08,300
Let me start with a direct question.

418
00:20:08,300 --> 00:20:13,220
Speaker 1, what does procrastination mean to you in a work context?

419
00:20:13,220 --> 00:20:18,580
For me, procrastination is when I delay an important task, even though I know it needs

420
00:20:18,580 --> 00:20:19,580
to be done.

421
00:20:19,580 --> 00:20:21,780
It's not just about being lazy.

422
00:20:21,780 --> 00:20:24,900
Sometimes I'm busy, but I'm working on less important things.

423
00:20:24,900 --> 00:20:27,460
That's a very important distinction.

424
00:20:27,460 --> 00:20:31,140
You're not doing nothing, but you're avoiding the right thing.

425
00:20:31,140 --> 00:20:32,140
Exactly.

426
00:20:32,140 --> 00:20:35,980
It often looks like productivity, but it's actually avoidance.

427
00:20:35,980 --> 00:20:38,660
Can you share a recent example from your work?

428
00:20:38,660 --> 00:20:44,400
Yes, recently I had to prepare a detailed performance analysis for a quarterly review meeting.

429
00:20:44,400 --> 00:20:47,160
It required data, charts, and insights.

430
00:20:47,160 --> 00:20:50,540
I knew it was important, but I kept postponing it.

431
00:20:50,540 --> 00:20:51,800
What did you do instead?

432
00:20:51,800 --> 00:20:57,320
I answered emails, attended minor meetings, organized files, and even updated documents

433
00:20:57,320 --> 00:20:59,060
that were not urgent.

434
00:20:59,060 --> 00:21:02,680
So you stayed busy but avoided the main task?

435
00:21:02,680 --> 00:21:03,980
Yes, exactly.

436
00:21:03,980 --> 00:21:06,720
And at first it didn't feel like procrastination.

437
00:21:06,720 --> 00:21:09,040
felt like I was being productive.

438
00:21:09,040 --> 00:21:12,280
That's one of the most dangerous forms of procrastination.

439
00:21:12,280 --> 00:21:16,000
I agree, because you don't immediately realize what you're doing.

440
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,560
So let's analyze this situation.

441
00:21:18,560 --> 00:21:20,880
Why do you think you delayed the report?

442
00:21:20,880 --> 00:21:23,320
I think there were three main reasons.

443
00:21:23,320 --> 00:21:25,680
First, the task was complex.

444
00:21:25,680 --> 00:21:29,160
Second, I wasn't confident with the data analysis.

445
00:21:29,160 --> 00:21:32,440
And third, I didn't have a clear starting point.

446
00:21:32,440 --> 00:21:34,240
Those are very common triggers.

447
00:21:34,240 --> 00:21:37,320
complexity, lack of clarity, and low confidence.

448
00:21:37,320 --> 00:21:41,920
Yes, and all three together made the task feel overwhelming.

449
00:21:41,920 --> 00:21:44,740
Let's talk about that feeling of being overwhelmed.

450
00:21:44,740 --> 00:21:46,760
What happens in your mind at that moment?

451
00:21:46,760 --> 00:21:53,040
I start thinking this will take too long, or I might not do it well, so I delay starting.

452
00:21:53,040 --> 00:21:54,560
That's very insightful.

453
00:21:54,560 --> 00:21:57,840
Procrastination is often emotional, not logical.

454
00:21:57,840 --> 00:22:00,560
Yes, it's about avoiding discomfort.

455
00:22:00,560 --> 00:22:01,560
Exactly.

456
00:22:01,560 --> 00:22:05,240
So the key question is, how do we reduce that discomfort?

457
00:22:05,240 --> 00:22:09,560
For me, the first step was breaking the task into smaller parts.

458
00:22:09,560 --> 00:22:11,320
Let's go deeper into that.

459
00:22:11,320 --> 00:22:13,480
How exactly did you break it down?

460
00:22:13,480 --> 00:22:20,640
I divided the report into sections, data collection, data cleaning, analysis, visualization,

461
00:22:20,640 --> 00:22:22,120
and final writing.

462
00:22:22,120 --> 00:22:23,400
That's very structured.

463
00:22:23,400 --> 00:22:28,160
Yes, and instead of thinking about the full reports, I focused on just one section

464
00:22:28,160 --> 00:22:29,320
at a time.

465
00:22:29,320 --> 00:22:31,280
How did that change your behavior?

466
00:22:31,280 --> 00:22:33,440
It made the task feel manageable.

467
00:22:33,440 --> 00:22:36,520
I told myself, just collect the data today.

468
00:22:36,520 --> 00:22:39,240
That felt easier than finish the report.

469
00:22:39,240 --> 00:22:45,000
That's a powerful shift, from a big abstract task to a small concrete action.

470
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:46,000
Exactly.

471
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,840
And once I started, I felt less resistance.

472
00:22:48,840 --> 00:22:51,480
That's something many people experience.

473
00:22:51,480 --> 00:22:53,160
Action reduces anxiety.

474
00:22:53,160 --> 00:22:56,120
Yes, starting is often the hardest part.

475
00:22:56,120 --> 00:22:58,120
Let's talk about another strategy.

476
00:22:58,120 --> 00:23:00,280
Did you use time-based techniques?

477
00:23:00,280 --> 00:23:02,980
Yes, I used focus time blocks.

478
00:23:02,980 --> 00:23:06,780
For example, I worked for 25 minutes without interruption.

479
00:23:06,780 --> 00:23:08,580
Like the Pomodoro technique?

480
00:23:08,580 --> 00:23:10,180
Yes, something similar.

481
00:23:10,180 --> 00:23:12,780
After 25 minutes, I took a short break.

482
00:23:12,780 --> 00:23:13,980
How did that help?

483
00:23:13,980 --> 00:23:16,180
It made the work feel less exhausting.

484
00:23:16,180 --> 00:23:18,980
I knew I only had to focus for a short time.

485
00:23:18,980 --> 00:23:21,180
That reduces mental resistance.

486
00:23:21,180 --> 00:23:22,180
Exactly.

487
00:23:22,180 --> 00:23:25,180
Let me share a different example from my experience.

488
00:23:25,180 --> 00:23:26,380
Sure, go ahead.

489
00:23:26,380 --> 00:23:30,540
I used to procrastinate a lot when I had to write client proposals.

490
00:23:30,540 --> 00:23:32,700
Why proposals specifically?

491
00:23:32,700 --> 00:23:38,380
Because they required both creativity and accuracy. I wanted them to be perfect.

492
00:23:38,380 --> 00:23:40,380
So perfectionism again?

493
00:23:40,380 --> 00:23:46,540
Yes, I would spend hours thinking about the best way to start, but I wouldn't actually start.

494
00:23:46,540 --> 00:23:47,900
That's very common.

495
00:23:47,900 --> 00:23:54,060
So eventually I changed my approach. I started writing a very simple first draft.

496
00:23:54,060 --> 00:23:55,340
What did that look like?

497
00:23:55,340 --> 00:24:00,060
It was messy, incomplete, and not very polished, but it existed.

498
00:24:00,060 --> 00:24:01,980
And that was enough to begin.

499
00:24:01,980 --> 00:24:06,380
Exactly. Once I had something, I could improve it step by step.

500
00:24:06,380 --> 00:24:09,420
So the goal was progress, not perfection.

501
00:24:09,420 --> 00:24:12,940
Yes, and that reduced procrastination significantly.

502
00:24:12,940 --> 00:24:15,100
That's a very important lesson.

503
00:24:15,100 --> 00:24:17,740
Let's talk about another real situation.

504
00:24:17,740 --> 00:24:21,020
Imagine you have a task with a deadline in two weeks,

505
00:24:21,020 --> 00:24:23,900
but you keep thinking, I still have time.

506
00:24:23,900 --> 00:24:26,060
Yes, that's a classic situation.

507
00:24:26,060 --> 00:24:27,340
What happens then?

508
00:24:27,340 --> 00:24:30,860
You delay starting because the deadline feels far away.

509
00:24:30,860 --> 00:24:33,820
And suddenly, the deadline is very close.

510
00:24:33,820 --> 00:24:37,180
Exactly. Then stress increases and you rush.

511
00:24:37,180 --> 00:24:38,700
How can we avoid that?

512
00:24:38,700 --> 00:24:41,980
One method is to create artificial deadlines.

513
00:24:41,980 --> 00:24:43,580
What do you mean by that?

514
00:24:43,580 --> 00:24:48,940
Instead of one final deadline, you set earlier deadlines for each part of the task.

515
00:24:48,940 --> 00:24:51,260
That creates urgency earlier.

516
00:24:51,260 --> 00:24:54,140
Yes, and it spreads the workload over time.

517
00:24:54,140 --> 00:24:56,300
That's a very practical strategy.

518
00:24:56,300 --> 00:24:59,740
Another thing that helps is visualizing the consequences.

519
00:24:59,740 --> 00:25:01,100
Can you explain that?

520
00:25:01,100 --> 00:25:03,740
I imagine what will happen if I delay.

521
00:25:03,740 --> 00:25:08,220
For example, stress, poor quality work, or negative feedback.

522
00:25:08,220 --> 00:25:09,980
That creates motivation.

523
00:25:09,980 --> 00:25:13,020
Yes, it reminds me why the task matters.

524
00:25:13,020 --> 00:25:15,500
Let's talk about workplace communication.

525
00:25:15,500 --> 00:25:19,180
Suppose you're procrastinating and your manager asks for an update.

526
00:25:19,180 --> 00:25:20,620
That can be stressful.

527
00:25:20,620 --> 00:25:23,020
What is a professional way to respond?

528
00:25:23,020 --> 00:25:27,660
I would say I've started working on the task and identified the key steps.

529
00:25:27,660 --> 00:25:32,220
I'm currently focusing on the analysis phase and plan to complete it by Friday.

530
00:25:32,220 --> 00:25:36,220
That's a strong response. It shows progress and a plan.

531
00:25:36,220 --> 00:25:40,220
Yes, even if you are behind, it's important to communicate clearly.

532
00:25:40,220 --> 00:25:42,220
What should you avoid saying?

533
00:25:42,220 --> 00:25:48,220
Avoid vague answers like, I'll do it soon or I'm working on it without details.

534
00:25:48,220 --> 00:25:51,900
Exactly. Specific communication builds trust.

535
00:25:51,900 --> 00:25:54,700
Let's talk about distractions in the workplace.

536
00:25:54,700 --> 00:25:57,420
Yes, they play a role in procrastination.

537
00:25:57,420 --> 00:26:01,500
For example, constant emails, messages, or notifications.

538
00:26:01,500 --> 00:26:02,860
How do you manage them?

539
00:26:02,860 --> 00:26:07,900
I schedule specific times to check emails instead of checking them continuously.

540
00:26:07,900 --> 00:26:09,500
That improves focus.

541
00:26:09,500 --> 00:26:14,300
Yes, and I also turn off unnecessary notifications during focused work.

542
00:26:14,300 --> 00:26:15,980
That's very effective.

543
00:26:15,980 --> 00:26:18,820
Another strategy is changing your environment.

544
00:26:18,820 --> 00:26:19,900
What do you mean?

545
00:26:19,900 --> 00:26:22,020
For example, if I work from home,

546
00:26:22,020 --> 00:26:25,380
I choose a dedicated workspace without distractions.

547
00:26:25,380 --> 00:26:27,540
Environment affects behavior.

548
00:26:27,540 --> 00:26:28,460
Exactly.

549
00:26:28,460 --> 00:26:30,420
Let's talk about energy levels.

550
00:26:30,420 --> 00:26:33,820
Sometimes, procrastination is not about the task,

551
00:26:33,820 --> 00:26:35,180
but about your energy.

552
00:26:35,180 --> 00:26:36,020
That's true.

553
00:26:36,020 --> 00:26:38,220
If you're tired, it's harder to start.

554
00:26:38,220 --> 00:26:39,620
How do you handle that?

555
00:26:39,620 --> 00:26:41,860
I try to schedule important tasks

556
00:26:41,860 --> 00:26:43,820
during my most productive hours.

557
00:26:43,820 --> 00:26:45,220
That's very smart.

558
00:26:45,220 --> 00:26:47,220
For me, that's usually in the morning.

559
00:26:47,220 --> 00:26:49,220
And less important tasks later.

560
00:26:49,220 --> 00:26:50,220
Yes.

561
00:26:50,220 --> 00:26:52,220
Let's explore accountability.

562
00:26:52,220 --> 00:26:53,220
How can it help?

563
00:26:53,220 --> 00:26:57,220
When you share your goals with someone, you feel responsible.

564
00:26:57,220 --> 00:26:58,220
Can you give an example?

565
00:26:58,220 --> 00:27:00,220
I sometimes tell a colleague,

566
00:27:00,220 --> 00:27:02,220
I'll finish this report by tomorrow.

567
00:27:02,220 --> 00:27:04,220
That creates pressure to follow through.

568
00:27:04,220 --> 00:27:05,220
Positive pressure.

569
00:27:05,220 --> 00:27:06,220
Exactly.

570
00:27:06,220 --> 00:27:08,220
Let's talk about rewards.

571
00:27:08,220 --> 00:27:10,220
Yes, they can be very motivating.

572
00:27:10,220 --> 00:27:12,220
What kind of rewards?

573
00:27:12,220 --> 00:27:14,220
Small rewards, like taking a break,

574
00:27:14,220 --> 00:27:19,660
Taking a break, having a coffee, or doing something enjoyable after completing a task.

575
00:27:19,660 --> 00:27:21,300
That builds a positive habit.

576
00:27:21,300 --> 00:27:24,740
Yes, your brain starts associating work with reward.

577
00:27:24,740 --> 00:27:27,180
Let's do a detailed scenario.

578
00:27:27,180 --> 00:27:28,180
Sure.

579
00:27:28,180 --> 00:27:33,260
Imagine you have a big presentation next week and you haven't started yet.

580
00:27:33,260 --> 00:27:36,540
First I would define the objective of the presentation.

581
00:27:36,540 --> 00:27:37,700
Good start.

582
00:27:37,700 --> 00:27:40,180
Then I would create a simple outline.

583
00:27:40,180 --> 00:27:41,180
Breaking it down.

584
00:27:41,180 --> 00:27:42,180
Yes.

585
00:27:42,180 --> 00:27:46,340
I would work on one section at a time using focused time blocks.

586
00:27:46,340 --> 00:27:47,700
And what about review?

587
00:27:47,700 --> 00:27:53,100
I would review and improve it gradually, instead of trying to perfect it in one session.

588
00:27:53,100 --> 00:27:54,620
That's a strong approach.

589
00:27:54,620 --> 00:27:57,160
And I would practice presenting it out loud.

590
00:27:57,160 --> 00:27:58,700
That builds confidence.

591
00:27:58,700 --> 00:27:59,700
Exactly.

592
00:27:59,700 --> 00:28:01,740
Let's talk about long-term change.

593
00:28:01,740 --> 00:28:02,740
Sure.

594
00:28:02,740 --> 00:28:05,840
How can someone permanently reduce procrastination?

595
00:28:05,840 --> 00:28:11,780
By building consistent habits, for example, starting work at the same time every day.

596
00:28:11,780 --> 00:28:13,220
creates structure.

597
00:28:13,220 --> 00:28:16,060
Yes, and structure reduces decision making.

598
00:28:16,060 --> 00:28:18,340
That's a very important point.

599
00:28:18,340 --> 00:28:20,820
Also, reflecting daily helps.

600
00:28:20,820 --> 00:28:22,660
What kind of reflection?

601
00:28:22,660 --> 00:28:25,340
Asking yourself, what did I complete today?

602
00:28:25,340 --> 00:28:27,180
What can I improve tomorrow?

603
00:28:27,180 --> 00:28:28,700
That builds awareness.

604
00:28:28,700 --> 00:28:29,700
Exactly.

605
00:28:29,700 --> 00:28:34,100
Before we finish, let's summarize the key ideas in a natural way.

606
00:28:34,100 --> 00:28:35,100
Sure.

607
00:28:35,100 --> 00:28:40,700
Procrastination often comes from feeling overwhelmed, unclear, or afraid of imperfection.

608
00:28:40,700 --> 00:28:43,980
The solution is to take small, clear steps.

609
00:28:43,980 --> 00:28:45,740
Break tasks into parts.

610
00:28:45,740 --> 00:28:47,900
Start even if it's not perfect.

611
00:28:47,900 --> 00:28:49,860
Manage your time and energy.

612
00:28:49,860 --> 00:28:51,380
Reduce distractions.

613
00:28:51,380 --> 00:28:53,540
And communicate clearly at work.

614
00:28:53,540 --> 00:28:55,100
That's a strong summary.

615
00:28:55,100 --> 00:28:58,060
Before we end, here's a question for our listeners.

616
00:28:58,060 --> 00:29:00,860
What is one task you have been delaying?

617
00:29:00,860 --> 00:29:03,820
And what is the smallest step you can take today?

618
00:29:03,820 --> 00:29:05,900
Try it right after this episode.

619
00:29:05,900 --> 00:29:07,540
Thank you so much for listening.

620
00:29:07,540 --> 00:29:09,860
We hope this conversation helps you become more

621
00:29:09,860 --> 00:29:11,900
productive and confident at work.

622
00:29:11,900 --> 00:29:15,500
Keep practicing, keep improving, and keep moving forward.

623
00:29:15,500 --> 00:29:16,540
See you next time.

624
00:29:16,540 --> 00:29:34,010
Goodbye, everyone.

625
00:29:34,010 --> 00:29:37,930
Hello and welcome back to Business English Podcast.

626
00:29:37,930 --> 00:29:38,770
Hi, everyone.

627
00:29:38,770 --> 00:29:41,810
Welcome to today's English Conversation Practice.

628
00:29:41,810 --> 00:29:42,770
I'm Daniel.

629
00:29:42,770 --> 00:29:43,930
And I'm Sophia.

630
00:29:43,930 --> 00:29:46,850
And we're really happy you're here learning with us today.

631
00:29:46,850 --> 00:29:49,610
On this channel, we help you speak English naturally

632
00:29:49,610 --> 00:29:52,370
and confidently through real conversations,

633
00:29:52,370 --> 00:29:56,050
practical examples and everyday workplace situations.

634
00:29:56,050 --> 00:29:59,570
That's right. And today we're going to talk about something that almost

635
00:29:59,570 --> 00:30:03,170
everyone experiences at work at some point. Yes.

636
00:30:03,170 --> 00:30:06,610
Today's topic is how to beat procrastination at work.

637
00:30:06,610 --> 00:30:10,930
This is a very real challenge for professionals in every industry.

638
00:30:10,930 --> 00:30:13,410
Absolutely. Whether you're working in an office,

639
00:30:13,410 --> 00:30:19,410
remotely, or even studying, procrastination can affect your productivity and performance.

640
00:30:19,410 --> 00:30:24,770
And the good news is, procrastination is something you can manage and improve with the right mindset

641
00:30:24,770 --> 00:30:31,650
and strategies. Exactly. In today's conversation, we're going to walk through real workplace situations

642
00:30:31,650 --> 00:30:36,450
where procrastination happens and how to deal with them using practical English.

643
00:30:36,450 --> 00:30:39,890
We'll also share useful phrases you can use in meetings,

644
00:30:39,890 --> 00:30:44,690
conversations with colleagues, and even when talking to yourself to stay motivated.

645
00:30:44,690 --> 00:30:47,570
That's a great point. So, let's begin.

646
00:30:47,570 --> 00:30:52,130
Daniel, let me ask you honestly. Do you ever procrastinate at work?

647
00:30:52,130 --> 00:30:58,770
I think if I'm honest, yes I do, especially when I have a task that feels difficult or unclear.

648
00:30:58,770 --> 00:31:03,410
That's very common. Many people think procrastination is just laziness,

649
00:31:03,410 --> 00:31:06,450
but it's often related to feeling overwhelmed.

650
00:31:06,450 --> 00:31:11,810
I completely agree. For example, last week I had to prepare a detailed report

651
00:31:11,810 --> 00:31:13,610
and I kept delaying it.

652
00:31:13,610 --> 00:31:15,250
What made you delay it?

653
00:31:15,250 --> 00:31:17,050
The task felt too big.

654
00:31:17,050 --> 00:31:18,610
I didn't know where to start,

655
00:31:18,610 --> 00:31:21,250
so I kept telling myself I would do it later.

656
00:31:21,250 --> 00:31:23,490
That's a classic situation.

657
00:31:23,490 --> 00:31:25,250
When a task feels too large,

658
00:31:25,250 --> 00:31:27,450
it becomes easier to avoid it.

659
00:31:27,450 --> 00:31:28,410
Exactly.

660
00:31:28,410 --> 00:31:30,690
Instead, I started checking emails

661
00:31:30,690 --> 00:31:33,450
and doing smaller, less important tasks.

662
00:31:33,450 --> 00:31:36,650
That's what we call productive procrastination.

663
00:31:36,650 --> 00:31:37,930
That's a great term.

664
00:31:37,930 --> 00:31:39,490
It feels like you're working,

665
00:31:39,490 --> 00:31:41,930
but you're actually avoiding the main task.

666
00:31:41,930 --> 00:31:45,050
Right, so how did you eventually handle it?

667
00:31:45,050 --> 00:31:48,450
I decided to break the task into smaller steps.

668
00:31:48,450 --> 00:31:50,730
Instead of thinking about the whole report,

669
00:31:50,730 --> 00:31:53,210
I focused on just writing the introduction.

670
00:31:53,210 --> 00:31:55,450
That's a very effective strategy.

671
00:31:55,450 --> 00:31:58,970
Yes, once I started, it became easier to continue.

672
00:31:58,970 --> 00:32:00,570
That's an important lesson.

673
00:32:00,570 --> 00:32:03,170
Starting is often the hardest part.

674
00:32:03,170 --> 00:32:04,490
Absolutely.

675
00:32:04,490 --> 00:32:06,290
Do you have any techniques you use

676
00:32:06,290 --> 00:32:07,890
to avoid procrastination?

677
00:32:07,890 --> 00:32:12,850
Yes, one technique I use is setting very clear deadlines for myself.

678
00:32:12,850 --> 00:32:14,970
Even if the company doesn't give you a deadline?

679
00:32:14,970 --> 00:32:15,970
Exactly.

680
00:32:15,970 --> 00:32:19,090
I create my own deadlines and treat them seriously.

681
00:32:19,090 --> 00:32:20,650
That sounds like a good habit.

682
00:32:20,650 --> 00:32:21,650
It is.

683
00:32:21,650 --> 00:32:26,210
For example, I might say, I will complete this task by 3pm today.

684
00:32:26,210 --> 00:32:27,770
That makes the goal specific.

685
00:32:27,770 --> 00:32:30,650
Yes, and it creates a sense of urgency.

686
00:32:30,650 --> 00:32:32,850
I think urgency is important.

687
00:32:32,850 --> 00:32:35,570
Without it, tasks can easily be delayed.

688
00:32:35,570 --> 00:32:36,650
Very true.

689
00:32:36,650 --> 00:32:39,530
Another strategy I use is prioritization.

690
00:32:39,530 --> 00:32:41,690
How do you prioritize your tasks?

691
00:32:41,690 --> 00:32:47,130
I ask myself, which task will have the biggest impact if I complete it today?

692
00:32:47,130 --> 00:32:48,890
That's a powerful question.

693
00:32:48,890 --> 00:32:53,370
It helps me focus on what really matters instead of small, easy tasks.

694
00:32:53,370 --> 00:32:57,730
I think many people avoid important tasks because they are more challenging.

695
00:32:57,730 --> 00:33:01,650
Exactly, but those tasks are often the most valuable.

696
00:33:01,650 --> 00:33:04,170
Let's imagine a workplace situation.

697
00:33:04,170 --> 00:33:08,110
You have a deadline coming up, but you haven't started the task yet.

698
00:33:08,110 --> 00:33:09,950
What would you say to your manager?

699
00:33:09,950 --> 00:33:10,950
That's a good question.

700
00:33:10,950 --> 00:33:13,190
I would be honest, but professional.

701
00:33:13,190 --> 00:33:17,990
I might say, I realize I need to make progress on this task, and I'm currently working on

702
00:33:17,990 --> 00:33:20,530
a plan to complete it efficiently.

703
00:33:20,530 --> 00:33:21,530
That sounds responsible.

704
00:33:21,530 --> 00:33:24,770
Yes, it shows awareness and action.

705
00:33:24,770 --> 00:33:25,970
What if you need help?

706
00:33:25,970 --> 00:33:31,150
I would say, I'd appreciate some guidance on how to prioritize this task.

707
00:33:31,150 --> 00:33:33,350
That's a great way to ask for support.

708
00:33:33,350 --> 00:33:37,150
Thank you. Asking for help can actually reduce procrastination.

709
00:33:37,150 --> 00:33:41,550
That's true. Sometimes we delay tasks because we feel stuck.

710
00:33:41,550 --> 00:33:45,150
Exactly. Getting clarity can make a big difference.

711
00:33:45,150 --> 00:33:50,750
Let's talk about another situation. You're working from home and you keep getting distracted.

712
00:33:50,750 --> 00:33:53,150
That's very common in remote work.

713
00:33:53,150 --> 00:33:59,550
Yes, there are many distractions like social media, household tasks, and even just feeling tired.

714
00:33:59,550 --> 00:34:01,150
How would you handle that?

715
00:34:01,150 --> 00:34:03,650
I try to create a structured schedule.

716
00:34:03,650 --> 00:34:05,050
What does that look like?

717
00:34:05,050 --> 00:34:07,450
I divide my day into time blocks.

718
00:34:07,450 --> 00:34:12,650
For example, I might work from 9 to 11 on one task, then take a short break.

719
00:34:12,650 --> 00:34:14,250
That's a great method.

720
00:34:14,250 --> 00:34:18,250
It helps me stay focused because I know exactly what I should be doing.

721
00:34:18,250 --> 00:34:22,750
I use a similar technique. It's sometimes called the focus session method.

722
00:34:22,750 --> 00:34:25,850
Yes, working in short, focused periods.

723
00:34:25,850 --> 00:34:29,950
Exactly, and during that time I avoid all distractions.

724
00:34:29,950 --> 00:34:31,630
That requires discipline.

725
00:34:31,630 --> 00:34:34,670
It does, but it becomes easier with practice.

726
00:34:34,670 --> 00:34:36,430
What about motivation?

727
00:34:36,430 --> 00:34:40,110
Sometimes people procrastinate because they don't feel motivated.

728
00:34:40,110 --> 00:34:41,710
That's a big factor.

729
00:34:41,710 --> 00:34:44,270
Motivation can change from day to day.

730
00:34:44,270 --> 00:34:47,230
So what can you do when you don't feel motivated?

731
00:34:47,230 --> 00:34:50,750
I remind myself of the purpose behind the task.

732
00:34:50,750 --> 00:34:51,950
Can you explain that?

733
00:34:51,950 --> 00:34:54,430
For example, if I'm working on a report,

734
00:34:54,430 --> 00:34:58,590
I remind myself that it will help the team make better decisions.

735
00:34:58,590 --> 00:35:00,830
That gives meaning to the task.

736
00:35:00,830 --> 00:35:05,390
Exactly. When you see the bigger picture, it's easier to take action.

737
00:35:05,390 --> 00:35:07,030
That's very insightful.

738
00:35:07,030 --> 00:35:10,510
Thank you. Another thing I do is reward myself.

739
00:35:10,510 --> 00:35:11,830
How does that work?

740
00:35:11,830 --> 00:35:18,190
After completing a task, I give myself a small reward, like a coffee break or watching a short video.

741
00:35:18,190 --> 00:35:19,630
That sounds motivating.

742
00:35:19,630 --> 00:35:23,870
It is. It creates a positive connection with completing tasks.

743
00:35:23,870 --> 00:35:28,150
Let's talk about teamwork. Can procrastination affect a team?

744
00:35:28,150 --> 00:35:33,150
Definitely. If one person delays their work, it can affect the entire team.

745
00:35:33,150 --> 00:35:35,450
That can create stress for others.

746
00:35:35,450 --> 00:35:38,050
Yes, and it can damage trust.

747
00:35:38,050 --> 00:35:41,250
So, how can you communicate if you're falling behind?

748
00:35:41,250 --> 00:35:44,550
I would say, I want to update you on my progress.

749
00:35:44,550 --> 00:35:48,050
I've encountered some delays, but I'm working on resolving them.

750
00:35:48,050 --> 00:35:50,050
That sounds honest and professional.

751
00:35:50,050 --> 00:35:52,550
Yes, transparency is important.

752
00:35:52,550 --> 00:35:55,450
What if someone else on your team is procrastinating?

753
00:35:55,450 --> 00:35:57,750
That's a sensitive situation.

754
00:35:57,750 --> 00:35:59,070
How would you handle it?

755
00:35:59,070 --> 00:36:00,870
I would approach it politely.

756
00:36:00,870 --> 00:36:03,950
For example, do you need any support with this task?

757
00:36:03,950 --> 00:36:06,270
I noticed it's still in progress.

758
00:36:06,270 --> 00:36:08,390
That's a very supportive approach.

759
00:36:08,390 --> 00:36:12,030
Yes, it avoids blame and encourages collaboration.

760
00:36:12,030 --> 00:36:14,110
Let's talk about mindset.

761
00:36:14,110 --> 00:36:17,510
Do you think mindset plays a role in procrastination?

762
00:36:17,510 --> 00:36:18,670
Absolutely.

763
00:36:18,670 --> 00:36:21,950
A negative mindset can increase procrastination.

764
00:36:21,950 --> 00:36:23,310
Can you give an example?

765
00:36:23,310 --> 00:36:26,390
If someone thinks this task is too difficult,

766
00:36:26,390 --> 00:36:28,350
they are more likely to avoid it.

767
00:36:28,350 --> 00:36:29,590
That makes sense.

768
00:36:29,590 --> 00:36:31,470
Instead, it's better to think,

769
00:36:31,470 --> 00:36:33,670
I can start with a small step.

770
00:36:33,670 --> 00:36:35,390
That feels more manageable.

771
00:36:35,390 --> 00:36:36,350
Exactly.

772
00:36:36,350 --> 00:36:39,270
Changing your thoughts can change your actions.

773
00:36:39,270 --> 00:36:41,070
That's very powerful.

774
00:36:41,070 --> 00:36:41,910
Thank you.

775
00:36:41,910 --> 00:36:44,030
Let's imagine another scenario.

776
00:36:44,030 --> 00:36:45,510
You have multiple tasks

777
00:36:45,510 --> 00:36:47,510
and you don't know where to start.

778
00:36:47,510 --> 00:36:49,350
That can feel overwhelming.

779
00:36:49,350 --> 00:36:50,550
What would you do?

780
00:36:50,550 --> 00:36:52,790
I would create a simple task list

781
00:36:52,790 --> 00:36:55,110
and rank tasks by priority.

782
00:36:55,110 --> 00:36:57,670
And then start with the most important one?

783
00:36:57,670 --> 00:37:02,150
Yes, or sometimes I start with a small task to build momentum.

784
00:37:02,150 --> 00:37:03,510
That's interesting.

785
00:37:03,510 --> 00:37:05,190
It depends on the situation.

786
00:37:05,190 --> 00:37:07,230
The key is to start somewhere.

787
00:37:07,230 --> 00:37:10,150
Starting seems to be a recurring theme.

